.TH ECVT 3
.SH NAME
ecvt, fcvt, gcvt \- output conversion
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
char *
ecvt(value, ndigit, decpt, sign)
double value;
int ndigit, *decpt, *sign;

char *
fcvt(value, ndigit, decpt, sign)
double value;
int ndigit, *decpt, *sign;

char *
gcvt(value, ndigit, buf)
double value;
int ndigit;
char *buf;
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ecvt
converts
.I value
to a string of
.I ndigit
digits, the last one rounded, and returns a pointer to it. The string
has no sign and no decimal point. The position of the decimal point,
counted from the start of the string, is stored in *\fIdecpt\fP; a
negative number means that it falls that many places to the left of the
digits. *\fIsign\fP is set to a nonzero number if the value is negative,
and to 0 if it is not.
.PP
.B fcvt
is the same, except that
.I ndigit
is the number of digits after the decimal point.
.PP
.B gcvt
converts
.I value
to the text printf's %g would print, with
.I ndigit
significant digits, places it in the array
.IR buf ,
with a \- before it if the value is negative, and returns
.IR buf .
.PP
The digits are those of the double's exact value, rounded correctly. An
infinity converts to "inf" and a not-a-number to "nan".
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
	p = ecvt(3.14159, 3, &d, &s);	/* "314", d 1, s 0 */
	p = fcvt(0.00125, 4, &d, &s);	/* "13", d \-2, s 0 */
	p = ecvt(\-2.5e\-3, 2, &d, &s);	/* "25", d \-2, s 1 */
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
printf(3)
.SH HISTORY
The
.BR ecvt ,
.B fcvt
and
.B gcvt
functions are in Version 7 AT&T UNIX.
.SH BUGS
ecvt and fcvt return a pointer to the same static array, which the
next call of either overwrites.
